<interrogationes unitas="vim">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I drive a vim-style editor over a character sheet?
    <responsum symbolum="vim_initiare vim_initiare_cum_contextu vim_tractare_clavem_cum_tempore TabulaCharacterum" via="lib/scriba_actiones.c">Make
      a TabulaCharacterum, create the state with vim_initiare (or
      vim_initiare_cum_contextu to attach a clipboard and undo stack),
      then feed every key through vim_tractare_clavem_cum_tempore and
      keep the VimStatus it returns. Printable keys are plain
      characters; arrows, Enter, Tab, Escape and the rest are the
      negative VIM_CLAVIS_* constants.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I treat vim_tractare_clavem as a pure function and keep the old status for my own undo?
    <responsum symbolum="vim_tractare_clavem VimStatus">No.
      Only the VimStatus struct is passed by value; the
      TabulaCharacterum, the VimClipboard and the VimUndoAcervus it
      points to are edited in place. An old copy of the status still
      points at the already-changed sheet. Read status.mutatus after a
      key to learn whether the sheet changed (yank never sets it).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does typing fd in insert mode leave insert mode?
    <responsum symbolum="vim_tractare_clavem_cum_tempore vim_tractare_clavem">fd
      is the house escape chord: an f followed by d within 0.3 s
      (FD_TIMEOUT) deletes the f and returns to normal mode. Pass a real
      clock in seconds to vim_tractare_clavem_cum_tempore. The plain
      vim_tractare_clavem passes time 0.0 for every key, so with it ANY
      f followed by d escapes, however slowly they were typed.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I share copy and paste between several editors?
    <responsum symbolum="vim_clipboard_initiare vim_initiare_cum_contextu VimClipboard">Allocate
      one VimClipboard, clear it with vim_clipboard_initiare and pass
      the same pointer to every editor's vim_initiare_cum_contextu. dd,
      yy, Y and visual d, x and y fill it; p and P paste below or above.
      With a NIHIL clipboard those keys still delete but nothing is
      saved or pasted. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): yy then p
      of a full-width 68-column line pastes only its first 67 columns.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can the clipboard hold long lines and big selections?
    <responsum symbolum="VimClipboard vim_tractare_clavem">Only
      up to its fixed size: VIM_CLIPBOARD_LINEAE_MAXIMAE (XXXII) lines
      of at most 67 characters (VIM_CLIPBOARD_LINEA_MAXIMA LXVIII
      minus a NUL), so the last column of a full 68-column line is not
      copied (the same 67-column cut applies to dd's undo copy). A
      visual y of more than 32 lines copies only the first 32, and a
      visual d or x of more than 32 lines DELETES only the first 32 -
      even with no clipboard attached. Copying is line-wise only: there
      is no y{motion}.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I undo any edit with u?
    <responsum symbolum="vim_undo_creare vim_undo_trudere vim_undo_tollere VimUndoAcervus">No:
      only line operations, and only with an undo stack from
      vim_undo_creare attached. In the code dd, visual d/x, p and P
      push entries; character edits (x, insert, backspace), o, O, d$,
      dG, J, &gt;&gt; and &lt;&lt; push nothing, although the header
      also lists o, O and dG. So after an unrecorded edit, u undoes an
      OLDER line operation instead.
      Each line is its own entry, so undoing a three-line paste takes
      three u. The stack is a ring of VIM_UNDO_MAGNITUDO (XXXII); older
      entries are overwritten. There is no redo. Caution (audit
      2026-10-18, unfixed): dd then u on a full-width 68-column line
      restores only its first 67 columns.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what keys and modes does this vim support?
    <responsum symbolum="vim_tractare_clavem vim_nomen_modi ModoVim">Three
      modes (vim_nomen_modi gives NORMAL, INSERT, VISUAL). Normal: h j k
      l, 0 $ ^, w b, g G (g alone goes to the top), i a A I o O, V
      (line-wise visual), x, dd d$ dG, yy Y, J, p P, u, &gt;&gt;
      &lt;&lt;; Escape in normal mode sets debet_claudere. Visual: j k
      g G, d x y, &gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;, Escape. Insert: printable ASCII,
      Backspace, Delete, Enter (splits the line with auto-indent), Tab,
      arrows, Escape or fd.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does G go to, the last line of text or of the sheet?
    <responsum symbolum="vim_tractare_clavem TabulaCharacterum" via="lib/vim.worklog.md">The
      last line of the SHEET (tabula-&gt;altitudo - 1), which is usually
      empty because the sheet has a fixed height. dG, in contrast,
      deletes from the cursor to the last line that has content. w
      also stops at the last line with content.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what happens when J joins a line that does not fit on the line above?
    <responsum symbolum="vim_tractare_clavem" via="lib/vim.worklog.md">Only
      whole words that fit move up; the rest stays on the lower line,
      pulled left to that line's own indentation, so nothing past the
      margin is lost. If not even the first word fits, nothing changes
      and mutatus stays FALSUM. Backspace at column 0 in insert mode
      joins the same way (fixed 2026-10-14; before that the overflow
      was cut off).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I insert a block of text programmatically without auto-indent?
    <responsum symbolum="vim_ponere_modum vim_tractare_clavem vim_tractare_clavem_cum_tempore VimStatus" via="lib/pagina.c">Set
      the mode with vim_ponere_modum(status, MODO_VIM_INSERERE), set
      status.sine_auto_indent = VERUM, send each character as a key and
      VIM_CLAVIS_ENTER for each newline, then restore the flag and the
      old mode (pagina_inserere_textum_crudus). Beware the fd escape:
      with vim_tractare_clavem (time 0.0 for every key, as pagina does)
      any f followed by d in the text leaves insert mode and the rest of
      the text runs as normal-mode commands. Use
      vim_tractare_clavem_cum_tempore with a time that grows by at least
      0.3 s (FD_TIMEOUT) per key.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
